-3 text-sm font-medium text-violet-600 dark:text-violet-400\">Read the analysis<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M5 12h14\"></path><path d=\"m12 5 7 7-7 7\"></path></svg></span></a><a class=\"group flex flex-col rounded-2xl border border-border bg-card p-5 transition-all hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:border-violet-500/40 hover:shadow-lg hover:shadow-violet-600/5\" href=\"/daily/linkedin-4-rules-for-ai-drafted-posts\"><div class=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-xs text-muted-foreground\"><time dateTime=\"2026-10-13\">Oct 13, 2026</time><span class=\"text-violet-600 dark:text-violet-400\">LinkedIn</span><span>#<!-- -->LinkedIn</span><span>#<!-- -->content creation</span></div><h3 class=\"mt-2 text-lg font-bold leading-snug transition-colors group-hover:text-violet-600 dark:group-hover:text-violet-400\">4 rules for AI-drafted posts that do not read like AI</h3><p class=\"mt-2 line-clamp-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground\">This week I fully reviewed an open-source toolkit for writing LinkedIn posts built on 2026 data. Four rules survived as the most usable: statement openings and number-first lines and links in the first comment and a real AI-tell scrub.</p><span class=\"mt-auto inline-flex items-center gap-1 pt-3 text-sm font-medium text-violet-600 dark:text-violet-400\">Read the analysis<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M5 12h14\"></path><path d=\"m12 5 7 7-7 7\"></path></svg></span></a><a class=\"group flex flex-col rounded-2xl border border-border bg-card p-5 trans